(notes "Buffet: All You Can Sheet. The front office is open for business: the game opens on the Series 777, the regulator's exam for a fund manager, eleven questions answered by a click or a key, ten of them scored, each right answer $500 more of the firm's $5,000, and the last the firm's style; then its certificate; then the Portfolio at Monday's opening bell, which waits for you to leave the exam room, the day, the hour and four stocks on it, each its price, its change since the last close and the holding of it, the firm's or your own, its shares, their cost, their value and the gain, an hour a second, the market open from 9:30 to 16:00 on weekdays and moving each half hour, and under them the cash on hand and the net worth, the firm's and yours, with the fine print at its foot. Beside it, Trading, where each stock's six buttons buy one share, ten or as many as the cash covers, and sell one, ten or all, at the price shown while the market is open, by a click or by a key: 1 to 4 pick a stock, then B, N and M buy and S, D and F sell, each also a command of the page's palette; and beside the cash, a switch, or C, turns whose cash an order spends, the firm's or your own, moving none of it. And Gigs, where a prospectus is proofread for $250, by a click on its button or by P, a day's work, one a week and four a run, Compliance permitting. Behind them, the back office: the market's calendar and its hours; its dice, rolled once a day where anyone can watch them roll; the market itself, four waves and a drift, and each session's hours between two closes; four stocks priced off it in whole cents, the dearest paying a dividend four days a year, paid to the shares held; the hustle's books, every prospectus counted; the proctor's books, the exam's draw and every answer; the order desk's books, every button and command and the order they make; the positions, each pocket's shares, cost and gains; the cash, the holdings and the net worth, the firm's and yours; the ledger, the last hundred orders filled; the fine print, a disclosure a month; the Series 777's question bank, twenty questions; the firms' four styles; and the model's caveats, the things this game gets wrong, each with Management's response. A returning candidate may waive the exam by writing 1 into Proctor!B3, at five correct answers. Pick the Day, the Dice, the Market, the Stocks, the Hustle, the Proctor, the Orders, the Positions, the Cash or the Ledger sheet to watch the model work, or the Caveats sheet to read, or the Disclosures, the Questions or the Firms sheet among the hidden sheets.")
